1. Docker
1.1 What is Docker?

Docker is a containerization platform.

Containers are lightweight, portable, isolated environments for running applications.

Unlike VMs, containers share OS kernel but have isolated user space.

1.2 Key Concepts
Concept	Description
Image	A read-only template for containers.
Container	A running instance of an image.
Dockerfile	Script to build an image.
Docker Hub	Public registry to store/pull images.
1.3 Dockerfile Example (Spring Boot)
# Start from official OpenJDK image
FROM openjdk:17-jdk-alpine

# Set working directory
WORKDIR /app

# Copy jar file
COPY target/myapp.jar myapp.jar

# Expose port
EXPOSE 8080

# Run application
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "myapp.jar"]

Build and Run
# Build image
docker build -t myapp:1.0 .

# Run container
docker run -p 8080:8080 myapp:1.0


✅ Now your Spring Boot app is running in an isolated container.

1.4 Common Docker Commands
Command	Purpose
docker ps	List running containers
docker images	List images
docker stop <id>	Stop a container
docker rm <id>	Remove container
docker rmi <image>	Remove image
2. Kubernetes (K8s)
2.1 What is Kubernetes?

Kubernetes is a container orchestration platform.

Manages deployment, scaling, and operations of containers in production.

2.2 Kubernetes Key Concepts
Concept	Description
Pod	Smallest deployable unit, one or more containers.
Deployment	Declarative updates for pods, handles replicas.
Service	Exposes pods internally or externally.
Namespace	Logical separation of resources.
ConfigMap / Secret	Store configuration or sensitive data.
Ingress	Entry point for external traffic.
2.3 Kubernetes Deployment Example (Spring Boot)
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: myapp-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: myapp
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: myapp
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: myapp
          image: myapp:1.0
          ports:
            - containerPort: 8080

Expose via Service
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: myapp-service
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: myapp
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 8080


Deployment ensures 3 replicas of your app.

Service exposes app at port 80.

2.4 Kubernetes Commands
Command	Purpose
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml	Apply deployment config
kubectl get pods	List all pods
kubectl get services	List all services
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>	Get pod details
kubectl logs <pod-name>	See container logs
kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml	Delete deployment
3. Docker + Kubernetes Workflow

Build Docker Image

docker build -t myapp:1.0 .


Push Image to Registry

docker tag myapp:1.0 <registry-url>/myapp:1.0
docker push <registry-url>/myapp:1.0


Create Kubernetes Deployment & Service

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f service.yaml


Scale Deployment

kubectl scale deployment myapp-deployment --replicas=5


Monitor Pods

kubectl get pods
kubectl logs <pod-name>


✅ This is production-ready container orchestration.

4. Tips for Spring Boot Apps

Use multi-stage Dockerfile for smaller images.

Externalize configs using ConfigMaps / Secrets.

Health checks: add /actuator/health and configure readiness/liveness probes.

Use Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) for scaling based on CPU/memory.

